UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 28, Lesson 3: Reported speech', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Reported speech tells what someone said without quoting every word, often shifting tense and using said that / told us, with clear attribution.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nReport: Sam said, \"The ticket is in my book.\"\n\nFEEDBACK\nSam said that the ticket was in his book.\n\nCHECK\nDropping who spoke makes reported speech useless as evidence.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nConvert six direct quotes to reported speech and three reported back to direct. Check attribution and tense.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Reported speech tells what someone said without quoting every word, often shifting tense and using said that / told us, with clear attribution.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and spelling","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-VOCAB-01","EN2-SPELL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Convert six direct quotes to reported speech and three reported back to direct. Check attribution and tense. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Sam said that the ticket was in his book. Reasoning: I used reported speech by attending to said that rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Dropping who spoke makes reported speech useless as evidence. Correction: Sam said that the ticket was in his book. Why: the correction restores accurate use of reported speech.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Dropping who spoke makes reported speech useless as evidence. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating reported speech on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with reported speech, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Sam said that the ticket was in his book. This matches the lesson skill for reported speech.","options":["Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Sam said that the ticket was in his book.","Dropping who spoke makes reported speech useless as evidence.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Report: Sam said, \"The ticket is in my book.\" Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Dropping who spoke makes reported speech useless as evidence. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Reported speech tells what someone said without quoting every word, often shifting tense and using said that / told us, with clear attribution.","Sam said that the ticket was in his book.","Careful use of said that with clear evidence.","Dropping who spoke makes reported speech useless as evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising reported speech?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named said that, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the said that used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising reported speech. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries reported speech.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show reported speech and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Dropping who spoke makes reported speech useless as evidence."],"question":"Read: \"In the dusty shed, Lila oiled the bike chain before the race.\" Which choice best uses reported speech from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Sam said that the ticket was in his book.","Dropping who spoke makes reported speech useless as evidence.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about reported speech?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise said that?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Dropping who spoke makes reported speech useless as evidence.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about reported speech.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of reported speech to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating reported speech on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Sam said that the ticket was in his book. Reasoning: I used reported speech by attending to said that rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with reported speech, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Dropping who spoke makes reported speech useless as evidence. Correction: Sam said that the ticket was in his book. Why: the correction restores accurate use of reported speech.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w28-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise reported speech.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use reported speech with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify said that and explain why it matters.","I can practise tense independently on a new prompt.","I can notice attribution needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Reported speech tells what someone said without quoting every word, often shifting tense and using said that / told us, with clear attribution. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Reported speech tells what someone said without quoting every word, often shifting tense and using said that / told us, with clear attribution."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about reported speech?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDirect: Mia said, \"I am late.\" Reported: Mia said that she was late. Keep who spoke clear.\n\nTRY FIRST\nReport: Sam said, \"The ticket is in my book.\"\n\nFEEDBACK\nSam said that the ticket was in his book.","explain":"Reported speech tells what someone said without quoting every word, often shifting tense and using said that / told us, with clear attribution.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Dropping who spoke makes reported speech useless as evidence. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Direct to reported speech shift.","caption":"Keep attribution clear.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Direct</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">&quot;I am late.&quot;</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Reported</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">said that she was late</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Direct: Mia said, \"I am late.\" Reported: Mia said that she was late. Keep who spoke clear. Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Direct: Mia said, \"I am late.\" Reported: Mia said that she was late. Keep who spoke clear. Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about reported speech?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDirect: Mia said, \"I am late.\" Reported: Mia said that she was late. Keep who spoke clear. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Tense shift contrasted with missing speaker.","caption":"Convert both directions.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"